.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt NANOSLEEP 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nanosleep
.Nd suspend the calling process for an interval
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In time.h
.Ft int
.Fn nanosleep "const struct timespec *req" "struct timespec *rem"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nanosleep
suspends the calling process for the interval in
.Fa req .
The
.Va tv_sec
field specifies seconds and
.Va tv_nsec
specifies nanoseconds.
Seconds must be nonnegative; nanoseconds must be from 0 through 999999999.
.Pp
The kernel rounds a nonzero interval up to whole 10 ms ticks and adds one
for the tick in progress, which is not counted, so a sleep that no signal
ends is never shorter than asked, up to the limit below.
The process blocks until that deadline tick; other runnable processes
continue to execute.
A zero interval returns at once.
.Pp
Once the deadline tick has come, the call returns 0 even if a signal is
pending; a pending signal that is not blocked is acted on as the call
returns.
If a signal that is neither blocked nor ignored is pending when the
process runs before its deadline, the call fails with
.Er EINTR .
Unless
.Fa rem
is null, the raw call stores there the whole ticks left to the deadline,
less the one added for the tick in progress.
A sleep begun again with a nonzero remainder has the first sleep's
deadline if it begins in the tick in which the remainder was stored, and a
deadline one tick later for each tick that passes before it begins.
A signal in the last tick before the deadline leaves a remainder of zero,
since the kernel counts whole ticks and does not record where in its tick
the sleep began; a sleep begun again with it returns at once, up to a
tick before the time first asked.
.Ss Differences from Linux
A stop signal that takes its default action, such as the terminal's
Ctrl-Z, ends the sleep as any other signal does.
The remainder is stored when the signal ends the sleep and the call
returns when the process is continued, so a sleep begun again with it
ends later than the first deadline by the time the process was stopped.
Linux resumes the sleep, which still ends at its first deadline.
Linux's remainder is exact, so a sleep begun again with it does not end
early; here the remainder counts whole 10 ms ticks, and a sleep begun
again with it can end up to a tick early.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call number is 162.
EBX points to the request and ECX to the optional remainder.
The kernel timespec consists of two signed 32-bit fields: seconds,
then nanoseconds.
The kernel limits the seconds field to 21474836, keeps the nanoseconds,
rounds the interval up to whole ticks, and limits the result to
2147483646 ticks, about 248.55 days.
.Pp
The raw call writes a zero remainder on normal completion.
If interrupted, it attempts to write the remainder but returns
.Er EINTR
even if that copy fails.
.Pp
musl converts its public timespec to the kernel's 32-bit structure; a
.Fa tv_sec
above 2147483647 is clamped before the call and the excess is added back
to the remainder on
.Er EINTR ,
so the public remainder need not equal the time left to the kernel's
deadline.
It copies the remainder back to the caller only on
.Er EINTR ;
on success, the caller's
.Fa rem
is unchanged.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn nanosleep
returns 0 on completion, or -1 on failure and sets
.Va errno .
The raw kernel call returns a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/timeprobe.c
requests a 150 ms sleep through system call 162 after forking a child.
It checks the elapsed interval and that the child made progress while
the parent slept.
.Sh ERRORS
The kernel handler returns:
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The request is null or cannot be read, there is no current process,
or the remainder cannot be written after normal completion.
The musl wrapper reads the public request and writes the public
remainder in user space.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A pending signal interrupted the sleep.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
Seconds are negative, or nanoseconds are outside the permitted range.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr gettimeofday 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2
